# kpmcore

KPMcore 26.08.1, the KDE partitioning library that Calamares (the installer, `packages/installer`) drives for every disk operation: it runs `sfdisk` to write partition tables, `mkfs` to make filesystems and `cryptsetup` to format encrypted volumes. Ubuntu's `kpmcore 26.08.1-0ubuntu1` (universe) rebuilt for @DISTRO_NAME@ with one patch, because an encrypted install in "Replace a partition" or "Install alongside" mode failed on the Legion laptop on 2026-10-11 (`docs/7 - image-installer.md`, choice 11).

The patch, `debian/patches/luks2-no-forced-sector-size.patch`, removes the `--sector-size 4096` that KPMcore 26.08 adds to `cryptsetup luksFormat`. cryptsetup refuses that option on a partition whose size is not a multiple of 4096 bytes ("Device size is not aligned to requested sector size"), and Calamares 3.3.14 aligns the partition only for "Erase disk" (Ubuntu's `align-end-sector-to-4k.patch`, Launchpad bug 2165428). Without the option cryptsetup chooses the sector size itself: 4096 on a drive with 4K physical sectors when the size allows it, 512 otherwise.

The package keeps Ubuntu's source name and binary names (`libkpmcore13`, `libkpmcore-dev`), and the version `26.08.1-0ubuntu1oaxaca1`: the policy's row for third-party software rebuilt from an Ubuntu source package. apt prefers that version over Ubuntu's `26.08.1-0ubuntu1` and drops it for Ubuntu's next upload (`26.08.2-0ubuntu1` or later), which is the intent: the fix is only needed until Ubuntu's Calamares aligns every partition it creates.

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `upstream` | the URL and SHA-256 of Ubuntu's `.dsc`; `scripts/build.sh` downloads the source package into `build/dsc/kpmcore/`, unpacks it, and lays this `debian/` over Ubuntu's |
| `debian/changelog.in` | our one entry, put on top of Ubuntu's changelog at build time |
| `debian/patches/series` | the one line appended to Ubuntu's series (Ubuntu's 26.08.1-0ubuntu1 has no patches of its own) |
| `debian/patches/luks2-no-forced-sector-size.patch` | the change, with its reasons in DEP-3 headers |

No fork of KPMcore: the orig tarball stays Ubuntu's, `debian/` stays Ubuntu's apart from the three files above, and `dpkg-source -b` applies the patch when the source package is built. Only `libkpmcore13` goes into the live root filesystem (`scripts/rootfs-create.sh`, the `live` profile); the installer's package removal leaves it on the installed system, where Ubuntu's next upload replaces it.
